From: Gemini PDA Linux bring-up
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: mediatek: mt6797: put vencsys/vdecsys/imgsys in their power domains

#85. These three clock providers program registers that live inside
MT6797_POWER_DOMAIN_VENC, _VDEC and _ISP, and nothing else in this kernel holds
any of them -- there is no video codec driver, and the camera only takes ISP
while it is open.

That was fatal in exactly one place. clk_disable_unused() and
genpd_power_off_unused() are both late_initcall_sync; the latter queues one
genpd_power_off_work_fn per domain onto pm_wq, so the power-offs run on other
CPUs while the clock sweep is walking the tree. Roughly half of all reboots,
the power-off reached VENCSYS first and the sweep's gate write never returned:
two CPUs hard-locked, /init never ran, and watchdog.open_timeout=120 reset the
machine 120 s later. Measured on build #119, from a trace that survives the
reset:

  clk-trace: previous boot's sweep DID NOT FINISH after 7 entries
  clk-trace:    6 disable   venc_3            <-- NEVER RETURNED

With patches/v6.6/clk/0011 the three providers enable runtime PM, so the clk
core takes a pm_runtime reference on them before touching any of these gates
and genpd cannot pull the rug out. This patch is the other half: without a
power-domains phandle there is no domain for that reference to hold.

camsys is deliberately NOT included. The sweep touches none of its gates, so it
is not exposed to this race, and its consumers already take the ISP domain
themselves (see the comment above the node) -- which is load-bearing for #66.

--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/mediatek/mt6797.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/mediatek/mt6797.dtsi
@@ -914,18 +914,51 @@
 		compatible = "mediatek,mt6797-imgsys", "syscon";
 		reg = <0 0x15000000 0 0x1000>;
 		#clock-cells = <1>;
+		/*
+		 * #85: these registers are inside MT6797_POWER_DOMAIN_ISP, and
+		 * nothing else in this kernel holds it -- there is no codec or
+		 * ISP driver bound. genpd_power_off_unused() and
+		 * clk_disable_unused() both run at late_initcall_sync, so
+		 * without this the sweep could write a gate register while the
+		 * block was being powered off underneath it. Measured: build
+		 * #119 wedged gating venc_3 with the CPU never returning from
+		 * the MMIO.
+		 */
+		power-domains = <&scpsys MT6797_POWER_DOMAIN_ISP>;
 	};
 
 	vdecsys: vdec_gcon@16000000 {
 		compatible = "mediatek,mt6797-vdecsys", "syscon";
 		reg = <0 0x16000000 0 0x10000>;
 		#clock-cells = <1>;
+		/*
+		 * #85: these registers are inside MT6797_POWER_DOMAIN_VDEC, and
+		 * nothing else in this kernel holds it -- there is no codec or
+		 * ISP driver bound. genpd_power_off_unused() and
+		 * clk_disable_unused() both run at late_initcall_sync, so
+		 * without this the sweep could write a gate register while the
+		 * block was being powered off underneath it. Measured: build
+		 * #119 wedged gating venc_3 with the CPU never returning from
+		 * the MMIO.
+		 */
+		power-domains = <&scpsys MT6797_POWER_DOMAIN_VDEC>;
 	};
 
 	vencsys: venc_gcon@17000000 {
 		compatible = "mediatek,mt6797-vencsys", "syscon";
 		reg = <0 0x17000000 0 0x1000>;
 		#clock-cells = <1>;
+		/*
+		 * #85: these registers are inside MT6797_POWER_DOMAIN_VENC, and
+		 * nothing else in this kernel holds it -- there is no codec or
+		 * ISP driver bound. genpd_power_off_unused() and
+		 * clk_disable_unused() both run at late_initcall_sync, so
+		 * without this the sweep could write a gate register while the
+		 * block was being powered off underneath it. Measured: build
+		 * #119 wedged gating venc_3 with the CPU never returning from
+		 * the MMIO.
+		 */
+		power-domains = <&scpsys MT6797_POWER_DOMAIN_VENC>;
 	};
 
 	gic: interrupt-controller@19000000 {
